<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pashupatinath Temple</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pashupatinath Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as Pashupati, 'lord of animals', on the Bagmati river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Already a major shrine in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> period, it is the most sacred Hindu site in Nepal and draws pilgrims from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>; the present pagoda-style building dates from the late 17th century. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple.jpg" alt="Pashupatinath Temple" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bijay Chaurasia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pashupatinath_Temple-2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pashupatinath Temple stands on the banks of the sacred Bagmati River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and is dedicated to Pashupati, regarded as a manifestation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. It has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1979, one of seven monument groups in the designation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, which describes it as an extensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple precinct of temples, ashrams, inscriptions and images raised over the centuries. The complex covers 246 hectares and includes 518 small temples besides the principal pagoda-style temple. Hindu tradition holds that the shrine is the head of Shiva, whose body stretches to the Kashi Vishwanath Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The temple is the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and is considered pre-Vedic in origin. According to the Shiva Purana, Shiva hid underground as a bull while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a> searched for him, and reappeared with his head at Pashupatinath, hump at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedarnath">Kedarnath</a>, face at Rudranath, arms at Tungnath and navel at Madhyamaheshwar.</p><p>Its existence is recorded as early as 400 CE. According to the chronicle Gopal Raj Vamshavali it was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> king Prachanda Deva, while another chronicle says it was a linga-shaped shrine before Supuspa Deva built a five-storey temple there. A medieval king, Shivadeva, who reigned from 1099 to 1126, rebuilt it, and Ananta Malla added a roof. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> temple of the 14th century and the Guhyeshwari Temple, mentioned in an 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, stand nearby. The present form dates from a renovation in 1692, after termites and earthquakes had damaged the earlier structures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main temple is built in Newari pagoda style on a square platform, with two roofs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> covered in gold, four doors sheathed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and a gold pinnacle. Its main image is a stone Mukhalinga, a metre high, with faces in four directions and a silver base bound with a silver serpent. Only four priests may touch it, from the Bhatta group; the Rajbhandaris act as treasurers, caretakers and assistant priests. Maha Shivaratri and Teej are among the festivals held there. Buddhists also hold the site sacred, linking it with mahasiddhas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>, who in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> tradition meditated at its cremation ground.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pashupatinath Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Both Shaiva Hinduism, centred on <b>Pashupatinath</b>, and Buddhism flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/licchavi-kingdom-of-nepal">Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal</a></li><li><blockquote>Shaiva Hinduism, centred on <b>Pashupatinath</b>, and Newar Buddhism flourished side by side, and Newari began to be written.</blockquote><a href="/state/thakuri-dynasty">Thakuri dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
